				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/djoric" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(388108); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=388108&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608930896" alt="Djoric" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=388108)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/djoric" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(388108); return false;" >Djoric</a></span> <span class="odate time_1346283828 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">29 Aug 2012 23:43</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1551100">
									

<p>A little of A, a little of B. First, and most importantly, they were a convenient and really cool way to explain why Able was the way he was, and then it just expounded from there: they gave him his powers and then kept him in captivity until they needed him to slaughter. The Mother was basically a mobile colony builder who never got used once they settled her down. A lot of other scips of various degrees were created by them as well. Basically, they're like what a lot of people consider the Chaos Insurgency to be like, except the Daevas actually do things.</p>
<p>Their propensity for keeping godlike entities within spatially-odd capsules basically makes them ancient evil Pokemon trainers with a team full of legendaries.</p>
<p>As far as building up, I can't say. Generally speaking, if something is big, they have a hand in it somewhere. Maybe the next interlude will be back in the day&#8230;</p>

							</div>

								
